  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The equation ${x}^{2}-6x+34=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+34 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x & =-34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =-34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9 & =-34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-25,$ and $p+q=-28.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 5\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-5\\,\\text{i}.$",
